++# FAA Commission follow-up: production accounts, release restrictions and unfinished original-record work
++
++**New primary-source acquisition, partial comparison; existing B129 / PD01–PD04 remain open.** Five PDFs were obtained from NARA’s public catalog and preserved unchanged with source URLs, access times and SHA-256 pins. This is a first review of three complete records and selected identifying pages in two additional packages. It has **not** recovered the corrected May2003 answer, its delivery receipt, a signed2006 action/no-action decision, or the recommendation-disposition records.
++
++The clearest new lead is an FAA telephone-call record attributing restrictions on some releases to headquarters litigation personnel and regional counsel. It also reports substantial production. A separate note reports unproduced materials and insists on the complete accident files. These accounts are retained together; neither has been selected as controlling and their causes remain unresolved. The originals behind the recorded instructions, requests and deliveries are the next useful evidence.
++
++## Sources and reading coverage
++
++All page numbers below are **one-based PDF pages**. Each F-number maps to an official download, acquisition time, entity hash, page count and reading coverage in [SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json). Exact selected text-layer lines are in [SOURCE-EXCERPTS.json](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.json); OCR errors remain visible. Line numbers are page-local extraction locators, not original agency line numbering.
++
++| Pin | Official record | Pages | Actual visual coverage |
++|---|---|---:|---|
++| F01 | [AWA673, Final Follow-up Response, NAID7601973](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7601973) | 2 | Both pages |
++| F02 | [AWA661, Record of Telephone Calls, NAID7601970](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7601970) | 1 | Entire page |
++| F03 | [AWA660, Note—9/11 Commission File, NAID7596181](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7596181) | 4 | All four pages |
++| F04 | [AWA658, FAA Issues and Questions Package, NAID7596179](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7596179) | 72 | Pages1–3 only; remaining69 detailed page reviews pending |
++| F05 | [AWA586, Email—Final FAA Testimony, NAID7601963](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7601963) | 11 | Pages1,2,11 only; remaining8 detailed page reviews pending |
++
++Total acquired: **five PDF entities,90 pages,3,106,377 bytes**. Actual visual reading:13pages. Text extraction of90pages is not reading90pages. The original protocol’s requirement to inspect every acquired page remains **unmet for77 pages in the added packages**; it has not been waived or redefined as a passing check. Acquisition and partial findings can be reported without claiming that the enlarged review or five-track goal is complete. Several components may repeat material held elsewhere; no independence or unique-evidence count is inferred from the number of PDF files. Raw scans, signatures, personnel/contact/health information and complete extractions stay local; these public notes link to the official sources.
++
++## O01 — The FAA call record reports production and restrictions
++
++The [AWA661 PDF](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-AWA-661-Record-of-Telephone-Calls-9-11-Commission-Followup-.pdf#page=1) is headed Record of Telephone Calls /9/11Commission Follow-up /AEA/ANE/AGL Regions and dated **10-03-03,1500–1600EDT**. Reading that printed date as October3,2003 is an interpretation of its format; the original string is preserved here. It records regional officials’ accounts, not an audio recording of those calls or the underlying legal instructions.
++
++- **AEA / New York:** the note says the Commission received a complete accident-file index, copies of all requested information and controller statements; the facility felt all requests had been met. This is affirmative production evidence in the record and must not be omitted. F02,p1,extracted lines7–12.
++- **AGL:** the note says the Commission did not receive the entire accident file; approximately40 requested pieces were copied and sent for release. It says the facility received guidance from **AAT-200 litigation personnel to release all data concerning UAL93 but nothing else**. It separately reports that the Commission sought field copies of headquarters telecons because headquarters was not releasing them. That latter claim has additional attribution layers: the note-taker records a regional official relaying the Commission’s position. F02,p1,lines13–26.
++- **ANE / Boston:** the note says interviewers learned about material in the accident file absent from the formal accident package. It lists QA bulletins, lessons learned, sector maps and frequencies supplied, while saying some requested material was not released because of **ANE legal guidance about information on hold by the FBI**. F02,p1,lines30–35.
++
++**What this establishes:** a publicly archived contemporaneous-looking FAA record reports particular release restrictions, partial production and claimed compliance. **What it does not establish:** the exact original instruction, its legal basis, what each item contained, whether another channel supplied it, final completeness, unlawful withholding or motive. No signed action/no-action decision is attached. Obtain the actual instructions, itemized inventories and receiving records before inferring their effect.
++
++## O02 — A separate note disputes production completeness and request scope
++
++The [AWA660 PDF](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/1-AAL-77/e-Other/1-AWA-660-Note-9-11-Commission-File.pdf#page=1) is addressed to **Shirley** and ends **Dana** onp3. It has no verified complete sender header, native email metadata or exact date. The phrase **middle of October** appears onp2. October2003 is contextual inference from the2003 request discussion, not an authenticated exact sending date. Do not silently assign a full author identity or treat this scan as proof of email delivery.
++
++The note quotes a May7,2003 request, due May14, for all FAA tracking documents including NORAD communications. It cites DOTRequest2,items3,7,16 and19 as additional examples, while expressly retaining the broader original request. It says managers/QA officials at **ZBW,ZNY,ZID,ZOB** reported never having been asked to gather these materials or to produce them from headquarters. These are statements in one note, not four independently acquired center affidavits. F03,p1,lines5–37;p2,lines1–9;p3,lines2–4.
++
++Its next steps insist on the complete **accident file**, rather than the **accident package**; all three Boston boxes if those contain relevant material; and all FAA facilities, not merely the four visited. It specifically identifies a Cleveland management memo, the Indianapolis outside-contractor review of the Flight77 radar-coverage lapse, and centers’ SATORI reconstructions. F03,p2,lines11–32. Page4 is a handwritten locator sheet; authorship, date and unclear characters remain unverified. A handwritten recording lead is not proof the recording was made, preserved or supplied.
++
++**Preserved difference:** AWA661 records facility-level claimed compliance and some productions; AWA660 describes unproduced materials and rejects a narrower file/package reading. Their dates, speakers, request scopes and later production sequence are not established as identical. We do not smooth them into one account, label one false, or infer concealment. Original requests and amendments, HQ-to-center tasking, actual legal guidance, center inventories, release manifests and receiving acknowledgments would test the relationship.
++
++## O03 — “Final” interview follow-up does not demonstrate universal completion
++
++The [AWA673 memo](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/dc-metro/rg-237/7419198-9-11-faa/5-Miscellaneous/e-Other/5-AWA-673-Memo-Presidential-9-11-Commission-Final-Follow-up-Response.pdf#page=1), dated **November7,2003**, runs from Boston ARTCC,ZBW-1 to Evaluations and Investigations,AAT-20. Its subject says **Presidential9/11 Commission Final Follow-up Response**; its opening says the information **should complete** outstanding requirements under an **October8,2003 AAT-1 faxed memorandum**. It explicitly concerns interviews of employees who worked on9/11. F01,p1,lines5–19.
++
++A manual count of the visible entries, retaining all statuses without publishing personal details, is:
++
++| Source group | People listed | Entries literally marked Completed | Other status descriptions |
++|---|---:|---:|---:|
++| Retired, p1 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
++| Changed facility/relocated, p1 | 12 | 9 | 3 |
++| Separate prose entry, p2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |
++| Leave/logistics table, p2 | 12 | 12 | 0 |
++| **Listed total** | **31** | **27** | **4** |
++
++These are30table rows plus one prose entry. They are not a workforce census or31uncompleted interviews. Nor does Completed establish that a new substantive account was obtained: group headings also say no additional information was provided. The conditional completion wording, Final subject, completed statuses and other statuses are all preserved. This tension warrants checking the referenced directive and later follow-up; it does **not** establish refusal, concealment or a contradiction in a signed2006 disciplinary outcome. The other status details are not republished because they include personal information unnecessary to the research claim.
++
++**Next original:** the October8,2003 AAT-1 fax, its recipients and enclosures, underlying interview notes/status definitions and subsequent follow-up. This memo is **not** the corrected May2003 QFR or its receipt.
++
++## O04 — Keep recording quantities in their actual units
++
++F02 records **over3,088 hours** of information, **6recorders×40channels**, and a **354days-of-manpower** estimate. F03 says the requested collection would not require review of **3000+phone lines**. These are different units and attributed estimates. We have not acquired a channel-by-time inventory, verified the workload estimate, established matched scopes or demonstrated a numerical contradiction. Preserve each value and its source rather than divide or equate them to manufacture one. F02,p1,lines16–24;F03,p2,lines33–40.
++
++## O05–O06 — Additional packages acquired; association and detailed review remain limited
++
++**F05:** the file titled Final FAA Testimony contains visible email pages dated **September20,2001,2:47PM** and **September24,2001,4:17PM**, referring respectively to Administrator and Deputy Administrator testimony. F05,p1,lines1–2,17–19;p2,lines2–3,18–21. These are different2001 emails, not two versions of a May2003 correction. The PDF includes an ensuing statement headed September25,2001 in its extracted text, but that heading has not yet been visually checked. Attachment icons do not authenticate recovered native Word documents or establish that every referenced attachment survives in this PDF. We have not compared every testimony page or authenticated a final submitted version.
++
++**F04:** the72page Issues and Questions Package opens with Flight77 radar/notification questions,p1; a DRAFT-marked communications transcript,p2; and an FD340 evidence-envelope form describing a **copy** of an FAA radar/communications timeline,p3. The form has printed identifiers **1A-1A76869** and **M-BSA-00006682**. Those are concrete custody/locator leads, not raw-audio authentication. Pages4–72 await detailed review. Extracting all text layers does not establish the package’s completeness, internal joins, newness of components, correct clocks or a corrected QFR. Do not count copied transcripts, timelines and their envelope as independent corroboration.
++
++## What changes, what remains open
++
++This pass moves from search-result titles to five preserved official PDF entities and fully reads three short records. It identifies specific release-instruction, tasking, interview and source-envelope targets. It does **not** close **PD01–PD04**, establish a deliberate cover-up, authenticate an FAA correction’s actual delivery, resolve conflicting accounts, complete a recorder reconstruction or validate a materials/steel hypothesis. A complete enlarged review remains pending77pages. Other tracks retain their previously documented dependencies; [OPEN-RECORDS.md](OPEN-RECORDS.md) carries them forward.
++
++A cover-up hypothesis would gain support from authenticated instructions or communications showing knowing suppression or alteration and actual withheld material inconsistent with representations. It would be weakened by contemporaneous complete transmissions, coherent lawful handling records, corrected answers and receiving acknowledgments. Neither account can be chosen from these scans alone. The strongest objection to treating the new material as proof of wrongdoing is that it records **disputed scope and channel-specific restrictions as well as production**, without the actual instructions, final disposition or complete receipt trail.
++
++[Method and limitations](METHOD.md) · [Claim ledger](CLAIM-LEDGER.json) · [Checklist](CHECKLIST.md) · [Revision accounting](REVISION-STATUS.json). Source integrity/software checks are separate from manual interpretation and publication verification. Earlier records remain unchanged; no outreach, request, fee, personal-mailbox access, case-spine edit, private push, deletion or history rewrite is authorized by this report.
